Fix: guest memory leaked in /dev/shm by every run that didn't shut down cleanly

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0005-shm-unlink-at-create.patch
Status:     local patch, not upstream yet (one line, POSIX only: worth proposing)

WHAT BROKE
  On Linux the 4 GB guest address space is a POSIX shared-memory object,
  /dev/shm/xenia_memory_<tick count>, created by Memory::Initialize through
  CreateFileMappingHandle (src/core/memory_posix.cpp). Its name was only
  removed (shm_unlink) in CloseFileMappingHandle, i.e. when Memory is
  destroyed. A run that crashed, was killed (timeout, Ctrl+C, a debugger),
  or exited without destroying Memory left the object behind, and with it
  every page of guest RAM the game had touched (~200 MB per run on the
  menus, more in levels). /dev/shm is a RAM-backed tmpfs: those pages stay
  in RAM (or swap) until reboot.

HOW WE FOUND IT (2026-09-25)
  After a day of test runs, launching the game died at once with SIGBUS,
  and other programs on the machine started crashing. RAM: 11 of 15 GB used
  plus 10 GB of swap, with no game running. /dev/shm held 83
  xenia_memory_* files (6.1 GB); no process mapped any of them
  (grep /proc/*/maps). The SIGBUS itself: touching a page of a tmpfs-backed
  mapping when the kernel can't provide one. Even a normally closed run
  after a reboot left its file behind.

THE FIX
  shm_unlink the name right after shm_open + ftruncate succeed. The object
  stays alive through the returned fd and the views mapped from it, and the
  kernel frees it when the process ends, however it ends. Nothing reopens
  it by name (MapFileView takes the fd; checked with grep over the SDK), and
  the later shm_unlink in CloseFileMappingHandle becomes a harmless no-op.
---
diff --git a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
index b52bf39..03c96a8 100644
--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -483,6 +483,14 @@ FileMappingHandle CreateFileMappingHandle(const std::filesystem::path& path, siz
     shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
     return kFileMappingHandleInvalid;
   }
+  // Remove the name right away: the object stays alive through this fd and
+  // the views mapped from it, and the kernel frees it when the process ends,
+  // however it ends. Nothing reopens it by name (MapFileView takes the fd).
+  // Before, the name was only removed in CloseFileMappingHandle, so every
+  // run that crashed, was killed, or exited without destroying Memory left a
+  // /dev/shm/xenia_memory_* file behind, holding the guest RAM it had touched
+  // (RAM-backed tmpfs, hundreds of MB each) until reboot.
+  shm_unlink(full_path.c_str());
   return static_cast<FileMappingHandle>(ret);
 #endif
 }
